    "ls|LS17_S10_U_135": "भारत सरकार \nजल शिÈत मंğालय \nजल संसाधन, नदȣ ͪवकास और गंगा संर¢ण ͪवभाग \nलोक सभा \nतारांͩकत Ĥæ न संÉ या *135 \nिजसका उ× तर 15 Ǒदसंबर, 2022 को Ǒदया जाना है। \n..... \nअटल भूजल योजना \n \n*135. सुĮी Ǒदया कुमारȣ: \n \n Èया जल शिÈत मंğी यह बताने कȧ कृपा करɅगे ͩक:  \n \n(क) È या अटल भूजल योजना राजè थान राÏ य मɅ Ǒटकाऊ भूजल Ĥबंधन को सुगम बनाने मɅ \nसफल रहȣ है और यǑद हां, तो त× संबंधी Þ यौरा È या है; \n(ख) राजè थान राÏ य मɅ अटल भूजल योजना के तहत लाभाͬथ[यɉ का िजला-वार Þ यौरा È या है; \n(ग) इस Ĥयोजन क े  ͧलए संè वीकृत और जारȣ कȧ गई धनराͧश का Þ यौरा È या है; और  \n(घ) राजè थान सǑहत इस योजना क े ͧलए चुने गए सात राÏ यɉ मɅ धनराͧश क े  ͪवतरण का \nÞ यौरा È या है?  \nउƣर \nजल शिÈत मंğी (Įी गजेÛ ġ ͧसंह शेखावत) \n(क) से (घ): ͪववरण सभा पटल पर रख Ǒदया गया है। \n \n***** \n  \n  \n\n“अटल भूजल योजना” क े  संबंध मɅ लोक सभा मɅ Ǒदनांक 15.12.2022 को उƣर Ǒदए जाने वाले \nतारांͩकत Ĥæन संÉया*135 क े  भाग (क) से (घ) क े  उƣर मɅ उिãलͨखत ͪववरण \n \n(क): अटल भूजल योजना को वष[ 2020 मɅ 7 राÏयɉ अथा[त गुजरात, हǐरयाणा, कना[टक, मÚय Ĥदेश, \nमहाराçĚ, राजèथान और उƣर Ĥदेश क े  जल कȧ कमी वाले चुǓनंदा ¢ेğɉ मɅ समुदाय के नेतृ×व मɅ सतत \nभूजल Ĥबंधन क े  उɮदेæय से शुǾ ͩकया गया था। इस योजना को राजèथान के  17 िजलɉ के 38 Þलॉकɉ \nकȧ 1140 Ēाम पंचायतɉ मɅ काया[िÛवत ͩकया गया है।  \n  \nअटल भूजल योजना का ǓनǑद[çट उɮदेæय समुदायɉ के नेतृ×व मɅ सतत भूजल Ĥबंधन करना है। इस \nयोजना क े उɮदेæय को हाͧसल करने क े  संदभ[ मɅ सतत भूजल Ĥबंधन को सुͪवधाजनक बनाने क े  ͧलए \nͪवͧभÛन जागǾकता काय[कलापɉ क े  माÚयम से समुदायɉ के åयवहार मɅ बदलाव लाया गया है।  \n \nराजèथान कȧ सभी 1140 Ēाम पंचायतɉ मɅ समुदायɉ को मौजूदा Ēाम और जल èवÍछता \nसͧमǓतयɉ क े  माÚयम से इकɪठा ͩकया गया है और सूͬचत तथा ͪवकɅ ġȣकृत Ǔनण[य लेने क े  ͧलए जल \nबजट मɅ भूजल आंकड़ɉ क े  संĒहण मह×व से संबंͬधत सूचना, ͧश¢ा और संĤेषण (आईईसी) और \nĤͧश¢ण काय[Đमɉ के  माÚयम से ¢मता सृजन ͩकया गया है। राजèथान मɅ समुदाय नेतृ×व वालȣ कुल \n1140 जल सुर¢ा योजनाएं (डÞãयूएसपी) तैयार कȧ गई हɇ, िजनमɅ जल बजट और Ĥèताͪवत मांग प¢ \nकाय[कलापɉ जैसे सूêम ͧसंचाई, फसलɉ का ͪवͪवधीकरण, पाइपलाइनɉ का उपयोग आǑद क े  साथ-साथ \nआपूǓत[ प¢ क े  काय[कलापɉ जैसे चेक डैम, फाम[ तालाब, ǐरचाज[ शॉÝट और अÛय कृǒğम पुनभ[रण/जल \nसंर¢ण संरचनाओं क े  बारे मɅ ͪवव रण शाͧमल हɇ। ͪवͧभÛन कɅ ġ/राÏय सरकार कȧ योजनाओं को एक \nसाथ जोड़कर ¢ेğ मɅ डÞãयूएसपी का काया[Ûवयन संबंͬधत ͪवभागɉ ɮवारा समुदायɉ कȧ सͩĐय भागीदारȣ \nसे ͩकया जा रहा है। \n \n(ख): चूंͩक, यह योजना सतत भूजल Ĥबंधन मɅ समुदायɉ कȧ सͩĐय भागीदारȣ पर लͯ¢त है, इसͧलए, \nबेहतर जल उपलÞधता और जल सुर¢ा क े  Ǿप मɅ समुदायɉ को इस योजना से ĤाÜत होने वाले लाभ \nसभी क े  ͧलए हɇ। इसके  अलावा, डÞãयूएसपी के अंतग[त Ĥèताͪवत कायɟ को संबंͬधत ͪवभागɉ ɮवारा \nउनकȧ è वयं कȧ Ǔनͬधयɉ के अͧभसरण क े  माÚयम से काया[िÛवत ͩकया जा रहा है।  \n  \n(ग) और (घ):  इस योजना क े  अंतग[त Ǔनͬधयɉ को मोटे तौर पर दो घटकɉ मɅ वगȸकृत ͩकया जाता है - \nसंèथागत सुǺढ़ȣकरण और ¢मता Ǔनमा[ण (आईएस और सीबी) िजसका उपयोग राÏय/िजले को ǓनयुÈ त \nकरने, िजला काया[Ûवयन पाट[नरɉ, Ĥͧश¢ण, आईईसी काय[कलापɉ, खरȣद आǑद और राÏयɉ को पूव[-\nǓनधा[ǐरत लêयɉ को हाͧसल करने के उपरांत Ǒदए जाने वाले Ĥो×साहन मɅ ͩकया जाता है। इन दोनɉ \n\nघटकɉ के अंतग[त सभी 7 राÏयɉ क े  बीच आवंǑटत और जारȣ कȧ गई  Ǔनͬधयɉ का ͪववरण नीचे Ǒदया \nगया है (12.12.2022 कȧ िèथǓत के अनुसार)-  \n                                                                                                                                 (करोड़ Ǿ.) \nĐ.सं. राÏय आवंǑटत Ǔनͬध कुल आवंटन जारȣ कȧ गई Ǔनͬधयां जारȣ कȧ गई \nकुल Ǔनͬध \n  आईएस और \nसीबी \nĤो×साहन \nǓनͬध \n आईएस और \nसीबी \nĤो×साहन \nǓनͬध \n \n1 गुजरात 217.65 539.11 756.76 39.56 124.02 163.58 \n2 हǐरयाणा 252.67 470.52 723.19 40.75 97.38 138.13 \n3 कना[टक 194.51 1007.01 1201.52 60.21 77.93 138.14 \n4 मÚय Ĥदेश 103.62 210.92 314.54 29.96 58.46 88.42 \n5 महाराçĚ 188.26 737.51 925.77 25.37 111.97 137.34 \n6 राजè थान 164.68 1024.97 1189.65 45.61 118.37 163.98 \n7 उ× तर Ĥदेश 119.28 609.96 729.24 28.41 33.25 61.66 \nकुल 1240.67 4600 5840.67 269.87 621.39 891.25 \n  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_136": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 136\n(To be answered on the 15th December 2022)\n \nNEW GREENFIELD AIRPORTS IN THE COUNTRY\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the aims and objectives of setting up of new Greenfield airports across the\ncountry;\n(b) whether the Government has set any criteria for setting up of such airports and\nif so, the details thereof;\n(c) the details of the Greenfield airports approved and the works started during\neach of the last three years, State-wise;\n(d) the names of the States which have forwarded proposals for setting up of new\nGreenfield airports during each of the last three years and the current year and the\naction taken by the Government thereon;\n(e) the details of the targets fixed and achieved so far in this regard, airport-wise;\n(f) whether there has been a cost escalation due to the delay in the implementation\nof the Greenfield airports project; and\n(g) if so, the corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (g): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*136. SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH ALIAS\n PAWAN RAJENIMBALKAR\nSHRI SANJAY JADHAV\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement in reply to parts (a) to (g) of the Lok Sabha Starred (*) Question No. 136\nfor answer on 15.12.2022 regarding \"New Greenfield Airports in the Country\"\n \n(a) and (b): A Greenfield Airport is set up at a place where there is deficit of\ninfrastructure available for air transport services or the existing air infrastructure\nfor the place is constrained/saturated to meet the growing air traffic demand.\nGovernment of India (GoI) has formulated a Greenfield Airports (GFA) Policy,\n2008 which prescribes the procedure and conditions for development of new\nGreenfield airport in the country. As per policy, if any developer including the\nState Government desires to develop an airport, they are required to identify a\nsuitable site and get the Pre-feasibility Study conducted for construction of airport\nand submit a proposal to the Central Government for 'Site-Clearance' and 'In-\nPrinciple' approval.\n \n(c) to (e): Government of India (GoI) has accorded 'In-Principle' approval for\nsetting up of 21 Greenfield Airports namely, Mopa in Goa, Navi Mumbai, Shirdi\nand Sindhudurg in Maharashtra, Kalaburagi, Vijayapura, Hassan and\nShivamogga in Karnataka, Dabra (Gwalior) in Madhya Pradesh, Kushinagar and\nNoida (Jewar) in Uttar Pradesh, Dholera and Hirasar in Gujarat, Karaikal in\nPuducherry, Dagadarthi, Bhogapuram and Oravakal (Kurnool) in Andhra\nPradesh, Durgapur in West Bengal, Pakyong in Sikkim, Kannur in Kerala and\nDonyi Polo, Itanagar in Arunachal Pradesh across the country.\n \nOut of these, 9 Greenfield airports viz. Durgapur, Shirdi, Kannur, Pakyong,\nKalaburagi, Orvakal (Kurnool), Sindhudurg, Kushinagar and Donyi Polo,\nItanagar have been operationalized. One more Greenfield airport i.e. Manohar\nInternational Airport - Mopa, Goa  has been inaugurated on 11 December, 2022\nand is ready for operationalization.\n \nBesides, Government of India has also granted the first stage clearance i.e. site\nclearance for construction of three Greenfield airports namely Alwar in Rajasthan,\nSingrauli in Madhya Pradesh and Mandi in Himachal Pradesh. During the last\nthree years, Mandi Greenfield airport project in Himachal Pradesh has been\ngranted 'Site Clearance' in 2022.\n \n(f) and (g): The project cost for the Greenfield airports depends upon various\nfactors such as land acquisition, removal of obstacles, financial closure etc. Further,\nthe responsibility of implementation of airport projects including funding of the\nprojects rests with the concerned airport developer including the respective State\nGovernment (in case the State Government is the project proponent). Progress of\nthe Greenfield airport projects under implementation is periodically reviewed by\nthe Central Government.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_137": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*137\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.12.2022\n \n \nMARKETING STRATEGY FOR MSMEs\n \n \n*137. \n \n \nDR. AMAR SINGH:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government subscribes to the view that both online and traditional offline \nmarketing tactics have to be used together to create an effective marketing strategy for Mi\ncro, \nSmall and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs);\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the initiatives that are proposed to be taken by the Government keeping in mind that \nwhile making marketing strategies, it is also important to define target audience and niche to \ndevelop brand awareness; \nand\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWE\nR\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) to (c):  \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*137 FOR ANSWER ON 15.12.2022\n \n(a) to (c):  \n \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) and its \norganisations have taken several initiatives for MSMEs through online and offline modes to create \nan effective marketing stra\ntegy for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs). Products and \nServices offered by integrating online and offline marketing strategies\n \nhelp MSMEs to increase \ntheir market reach. These include:\n \ni\n.\n \nThe Procurement and Marketing Support (PMS) Scheme\n:  The sc\nheme is designed \nto promote new market access initiatives and enhance the marketability of products and \nservices in the MSME sector. The scheme aims to create awareness and educate the \nMSMEs about importance / methods/ process of packaging in marketing, im\nport\n-\nexport \npolicy and procedure, trade fairs and digital advertising, GeM portal, MSME Conclave, \nlatest developments \nin international/national trade.\n \n \nii\n.\n \nInternational Cooperation Scheme\n: The scheme aims to capacity build MSMEs for \nentering export market by \nfacilitating their participation in international \nexhibitions/trade fairs abroad, organizing international conferences both physically and \nvirtually.  A new component of the scheme namely Capacity Building of First Time \nExporters (CBFTE) has been launched \non 30\nth\n \nJune, 2022 for reimbursement of various \ncosts incurred by Micro and Small Enterprises towards export of goods and services. \n \n \niii\n.\n \nKhadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC): \nFor promotion of online \nmarketing of Khadi\n \nand Village Industries products through e\n-\nmarket linkages to \nMSMEs, KVIC has also developed an online portal ‘\nwww.ekhadiindia.com\n’.  KVIC \nalso promotes /supports Small and Micro enterprises in marketing of Khadi an\nd Village \nIndustries products through network of its sale outlets spread across the country.   \n \n \niv\n.\n \nCoir Board\n: The Coir Board is granting Market Development Assistance (MDA) to the \nMSMEs and co\n-\noperatives through the concerned State Governments to the tune o\nf \n10% of average sales. The Board has already registered its 29 Showrooms under GeM \nPortal.  Smart phones have been provided to Board’s Showrooms to do the transactions \nin a digital mode. All the coir units are required to capture sales through reputed on\n-\nline/ e\n-\ncommerce platforms.\n \n \nv\n.\n \nNational Small Industries Corporation (NSIC) Limited\n: \nNSIC has developed \nBusiness to Business (B2B) portal viz. ‘MSME Global Mart to enhance business of \nMSMEs. The key features and services of the portal include tender informat\nion, trade \nleads, member’s homepage, featured product catalogue, request for quotation. \n \n \nAlso, under the National Scheduled Caste\n–\nScheduled Tribe Hub (NSSH) Scheme, several \ninterventions/sub\n-\nschemes have been introduced in consultation with various stakeh\nolders for \ncapacity building, financial assistance, and market linkage of the SC\n-\nST entrepreneurs. The \ninterventions for market linkages aims at visibility of the SC\n-\nST owned MSEs and their \nparticipation in the public procurement so as to achieve the manda\nte of the Public Procurement \nPolicy of the Government. \n \n*****\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_138": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*138  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.12.2022 \n \nSujalam Project \n \n*138. SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has recently launched the Sujalam 2.0 greywater recycling \nproject and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the total amount of greywater discharged in the country annually, State/UT -wise \nincluding Saran district of Bihar; \n \n(c) the to tal number of greywater recycling plants that are presently functional in the \ncountry, State/UT-wise including Saran district of Bihar; \n \n(d) the amount of greywater that has been recycled during the last one year, State/UT-wise \nincluding Bihar and the estimated cost incurred; and \n \n(e) whether the Government has any policy to financially help the States with larger \ngreywater recycling requirements and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n \n(a) to (e)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in the reply to Lok  Sabha Starred Question No. *138 due for \nreply on 15.12.2022 \n \n(a) to (e)  Under Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [SBM -G] Phase II, management of \ngreywater is promoted through the on-site systems near the source, wherever possible, such \nas soak pits/leach pits/magic pits at household or at community level.  However, on a larger \nscale or at the village level, the greywater can be managed through specialized technologies \nviz., Waste Stabilization Ponds  (WSPs), Constructed Wetlands, etc., as per the feasibility.  \nTo give impetus to greywater management in rural areas , Suj alam campaigns have been \ncarried out across the country.  Suj alam 2.0, a 100 days campaign, was launched on the \nWorld Water Day, 22 nd March 2022.  Under the campaign the focus was on prioritizing \ngreywater management through households/community level soak pits/leach pits/magic \npits.  The campaign concluded on 30 th June, 2022.  A s reported by the States/UTs, more \nthan 12 lakh household and community soak pits/leach pits/magic pits  were constructed \nunder Sujalam 2.0. \nAs on 12 th December, 2022, over 6.82 lakhs community soakpit s/leach pits/magic \npits and 4 2.17 lakhs household soakpits /leach pits/magic pits/kitchen gardens  have been \nbuilt throughout the country , as reported by the States/UTs .  Further, around 3 2,989 other \ncommunity level greywater management systems , such as WSPs, Constructed Wetlands, \netc. have been constructed.  State/UT-wise details are given at Annexure-1.   \nIn Saran district of Bihar, 803 community soak pits/leach pits/magic pits, 3934 \nhousehold soak pits/leach pits/magic pits/kitchen gardens and 32 other community \ngreywater management systems are reported to have been constructed.   \nThe data regarding the amount of greywater discharged or recycled in rural areas is \nnot being maintained. \nAs per SBM -G guidelines, for greywater management in larger villages with more \nthan 5000 population, there  is a provision for financial assistance upto Rs. 660 per capita \n(including State share) for construction of greywater management systems.  Further  more, \nin addition, the States can also utilise MGNERGS funds , 15th Finance Commission grants \nfor Rural Local Bodies, MPLAD, funds, etc. \n  \n\nAnnexure-1 \nState/UT-wise details of household and community soak pits/ leach pits/ magic pits \nand other community greywater management systems  such as WSPs etc , reported to \nhave been constructed till 12.12.2022 \nSl. \nNo \nState/UT No. of \ncommunity soak \npits /leach \npits/magic pits \n \nNo. of household \nsoak pits/ leach \npits/ magic pits/ \nkitchen gardens  \nNo. of other \ncommunity \ngreywater \nmanagement \nsystems such as \nWSPs, etc. \n1 A & N Islands 63 164 19 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 2,062 4,07,976 570 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1,573 4,563 236 \n4 Assam 845 730 4 \n5 Bihar 32,220 1,61,395 2,251 \n6 Chhattisgarh 52,195 31,947 1,430 \n7 D & N Haveli and Daman & Diu 43 217 47 \n8 Goa 53 1,060 24 \n9 Gujarat 10,205 83,523 464 \n10 Haryana 6,116 4,557 2,376 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 1,749 6,760 502 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 32,188 4,02,032 991 \n13 Jharkhand 14,986 12,490 1,360 \n14 Karnataka 923 5,85,351 346 \n15 Kerala 1,487 10,924 302 \n16 Ladakh 1 17,992 0 \n17 Lakshadweep 8 44 0 \n18 Madhya Pradesh 1,25,089 2,86,592 3,681 \n19 Maharashtra 12,944 3,14,683 1,864 \n20 Manipur 115 998 7 \n21 Meghalaya 233 40,653 34 \n22 Mizoram 220 3,375 0 \n23 Nagaland 413 7,411 78 \n24 Odisha 1,78,809 6,22,199 1,379 \n25 Puducherry 6 284 0 \n26 Punjab 86 3,398 730 \n27 Rajasthan 57,414 13,537 2,664 \n28 Sikkim 38 3,648 25 \n29 Tamil Nadu 21,963 2,79,074 950 \n30 Telangana 18,936 4,91,607 2,476 \n31 Tripura 698 3,754 36 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 94,907 2,25,669 6,450 \n33 Uttarakhand 2,551 14,571 152 \n34 West Bengal 11,500 1,74,300 1,541 \nTotal 6,82,639 42,17,478 32,989 \nSource: SBM(G) IMIS \n ",
