    "ls|LS17_S10_U_227": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 227\n(To be answered on the 22nd December 2022)\n \nOPERATIONAL CRITERIA FOR AIRCRAFT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that many of the private airlines\noperating in the country are technologically archaic and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) whether the Government has any criteria for determining the airworthy\nconditions of aircraft including its lifespan and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether several old aircraft of various airlines were unable to meet basic\noperational criteria and if so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n*****\n*227. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question\nNo. 227 regarding \"Operational Criteria for Aircraft\" to be answered on\n22.12.2022\n(a) & (b) The Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has not\nprescribed guidelines specifying life for an aircraft to fly in India. Aircraft is\nconsidered airworthy provided the maintenance is as per approved schedule\nlaid down by the manufacturer. Aircraft registered in India may operate as\nlong as the aircraft is under maintenance support provided by manufacturer\nfor the continuous operation of the aircraft.\nThe aircraft may be withdrawn from operation by an operator, in case it\nbecomes 'beyond economic repair' or is 'permanently withdrawn from use'\ndue to any reason such as unavailability of spares, etc.\nHowever, DGCA has laid down age criteria for an aircraft to be imported into\nIndia as under:\n(1) Aircraft intended to be imported and used in passenger services\n(Scheduled/ Non-Scheduled) and General Aviation\n(i) Pressurized aircraft restricted to 18 years of age or 65% of designed\neconomic life in terms of pressurization cycle, whichever is earlier\n(ii) Non Pressurized aircraft (intended to import in India) allowed up to 20\nyears of age on case to case basis agreed by DGCA.\n(2) Aircraft to be used for air cargo operations restricted to 25 years in age or\n75% of its designed economic life in terms of pressurization cycles, whichever\nis earlier.\n(c) No sir. No aircraft shall fly without having valid Airworthiness Review\nCertificate (ARC) as per Rule 15 of the Aircraft Rules, 1937. The\nresponsibility of the operator is to keep the aircraft continuous airworthy and\nin a controlled environment. The aircraft operating in India are periodically\ninspected for compliance with DGCA regulations.The aircraft can also be\nwithdrawn by the airline from flying when the spare parts and consumables\nto maintain the aircraft are not available from OEM & Manufacturer to keep\nit airworthy.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_228": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 228 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 22, 2022 \n \nFUND SHARING UNDER AMRUT SCHEME \n \nNO. 228.   SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of Atal Mission for Rejuvenation an d Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT) scheme including fund sharin g pattern \nbetween Centre and States; \n(b)  the details of the cities where AMRUT scheme i s running in the \ncities of Jharkhand;  \n(c)  the details of fund allocation and utilization  thereof in the State of \nJharkhand under the scheme, project and district-wise; \n(d)  whether there exists specific guidelines to mo nitor and supervise \nthe implementation of the scheme at district and State level; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof along with the moni toring committees \nconstituted for the purpose and the number of such committees’ \nmeetings held during the last five years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the  House. \n***** \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PART (a) TO (e) O F THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION No. *228 DUE FOR ANSWER IN T HE LOK \nSABHA ON 22 DECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING “FUND SHARING UNDER \nAMRUT SCHEME”: \n \n(a):   Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformat ion (AMRUT) \nwas launched on 25 th June 2015 for a period of 05 years i.e. Financial \nYear 2015-16 to 2019-20with a total Central outlay of ₹50,000 crore in \n500 selected cities across all the States and Union  Territories (UTs) in \nthe country. The Mission focuses on the development  of basic urban \ninfrastructure in the sectors of water supply; sewe rage and septage \nmanagement; storm water drainage; non-motorized urb an transport \nand development of green spaces & parks. Mission al so executed a \nset of 11 reforms to improve governance of Urban Lo cal Bodies (ULBs) \nand Capacity Building activities for the ULBs.  \n Under AMRUT, total approved plan size of all the S tates and UTs \nis ₹77,640 crore, which includes committed Central Assistance of \n₹35,990 crore. Through this, the Mission envisions to provide 139 lakh \nwater tap connections to achieve universal coverage  of water supply \nand 145 lakh sewer connections to enhance substanti al sewerage \nnetwork coverage in AMRUT cities. AMRUT has now bee n subsumed \nunder Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Trans formation 2.0 \n(AMRUT 2.0) launched on 01 \nst October 2021. Accordingly, all ongoing \nand eligible projects of AMRUT shall receive Centra l funding till \n31 st March 2023. \n\n Fund sharing pattern between Centre and States for  AMRUT \nprojects is as under: \nSl. \nNo.  \nCategory of \nState / Union \nTerritory \nSector  Category of \nAMRUT city in \nterms of \npopulation (as \nper 2011 census) \nMaximum \nCentral \nShare \nagainst \nproject \ncost* \nState/ULB/ \nOther’s \nshare \nagainst \nproject \ncost \ni. \nFor all Union \nTerritories \nAll Sectors  For a ll AMRUT \ncities \nirrespective of \npopulation \n100%  - \nii. \nFor 08 North -\nEastern \nStates and \nHimalayan \nStates \n(Himachal \nPradesh and \nUttarakhand)  \nAll Sectors  For a ll AMRUT \ncitiesirrespective \nof population \n90%  10%  \niii. \nAll remaining \nStates \nWater \nSupply, \nSewerage \nand Septage \nManagement, \nStorm Water \nDrainage and \nUrban \nTransport \n(non-\nmotorized) \nCities having \npopulation up to \n10 lakhs \n50%  50%  \niv. \nCities having \npopulation above \n10 lakhs \n33.33%  66.67%  \nv. Parks/ Green For all AMRUT \ncities \n50%  50%  \n\nSpaces  irrespective of \npopulation \n* Limited to maximum committed Central Assistance. \n \n(b):  List of 7 cities selected under AMRUT in the State of \nJharkhand is as under: \nS.No.  Name of the City \n1 Adityapur \n2 Chas \n3 Deoghar \n4 Dhanbad \n5 Giridih \n6 Hazaribag \n7 Ranchi \n \n(c):  Under AMRUT, Central funds are not allocated project or \ndistrict-wise. For the State of Jharkhand, State An nual Action Plans \n(SAAPs) amounting to ₹1,246crore, including committ ed Central \nAssistance of ₹566 crore have been approved for the  entire Mission \nperiod. So far, ₹552crore has been released to the State as Central \nfunds for implementation of projects under the Miss ion, against which \nState Government has submitted Utilisation Certific ates for ₹386 \ncrore. District-wise details of projects taken up u nder AMRUT in the \nState of Jharkhand and expenditure reported so far has been placed \nas ANNEXURE-I. \n \n\n(d) & (e): Under AMRUT, States have been empowered to select, \napprise, approve and implement projects within the broad framework \nof AMRUT Mission. Further, AMRUT guidelines have sp ecific provisions \nfor formation of State High Powered Steering Commit tee (SHPSC), \nheaded by the Chief Secretary of the State, to moni tor and supervise \nthe implementation of the scheme at the State level . Apart from this, a \nState Level Technical Committee (SLTC) headed by th e Secretary, \nUrban Development & Housing Department also exists to provide \ntechnical support to the SHPSC in monitoring and su pervising the \nscheme at the State level. For monitoring of AMRUT projects at \ndistrict level, AMRUT guidelines provide for consti tution of a District \nLevel Review and Monitoring Committee (DLRMC) which  will be co-\nchaired by the Member(s) of Parliament along with t he District \nCollector. In District Level a committee under the Chairmanship of \nmember of Parliament formed namely District Develop ment \nCoordination and Monitoring Committee for monitorin g of all \ndevelopment work at State Level. The committee also  review the \nAMRUT projects on quarterly and monthly basis. \nUnder AMRUT, 09 meetings of SHPSC, 11 meetings of S LTC and \n49 meetings at District level (Details at ANNEXURE- II) have been \norganized by the Government of Jharkhand during las t five years i.e. \nfinancial year 2017-18 to 2021-22. \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF STAR RED QUESTION \nNO. 228* REGARDING “FUND SHARING UNDER AMRUT SCHEME ”, DUE FOR \nANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 22 DECEMBER, 2022.  \nDistrict-wise details of projects grounded under AM RUT in the State of \nJharkhand  \nS.No.  District City \nProjects Grounded \nTotal \nNo. \nTotal cost  \n(In ₹ crore)  \nCumulative Total \nExpenditure* \n(In ₹ crore) \n1 Adityapur Adityapur 13 548.41 307.13 \n2 Chas Chas 10 142.26 69.08 \n3 Deoghar Deoghar 6 13.98 6.39 \n4 Dhanbad Dhanbad 6 164.31 55 \n5 Giridih Giridih 7 46.18 36.53 \n6 Hazaribag Hazaribag  9 428.41 232.89 \n7 Ranchi Ranchi 8 271.51 155.97 \nGrand Total 59 1615.06 862.99 \n* Cumulative total expenditure incurred so far on g rounded projects \nincluding Central share and share of State/ Urban L ocal Bodies (ULBs)/ \nOther agencies, as applicable. \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) OF STA RRED QUESTION \nNO. 228* REGARDING “FUND SHARING UNDER AMRUT SCHEME ”, DUE FOR \nANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 22 DECEMBER, 2022.  \nDetails of meetings of District Level Monitoring Committee in the State \nof Jharkhand  \nS.No.  District Urban Local \nBodies \nNos of \nMeetings held Dates of Meetings \n1 Ranchi \nRanchi \nMunicipal \nCorporation \n7 \n15.01.2018, 06.06.2018, \n10.09.2018, 29.12.2018, \n04.10.2019, 19.08.2021, \n20.08.2022 \n2 Bokaro Chas Municipal \nCorporation 7 \n06.05.2017, 14.09.2017, \n28.06.2018, 27.04.2018, \n08.12.2018, 07.08.2021, \n08.10.2022 \n3 Hazaribag  \nHazaribag \nMunicipal \nCorporation \n7 \n09.10.2018, 08.08.2019, \n12.09.2020, 13.03.2021, \n24.07.2021, 10.01.2022, \n22.06.2022 \n4 Dhanbad \nDhanbad \nMunicipal \nCorporation \n6 \n10.09.2016, 25.03.2017, \n10.07.2018, 12.02.2018, \n23.09.2021, 05.03.2022 \n5 Giridih \nGiridih \nMunicipal \nCorporation \n7 \n07.09.2017, 10.03.2018, \n26.03.2018, 10.11.2018, \n05.09.2019, 25.09.2021, \n05.05.2022 \n6 Deoghar \nDeoghar \nMunicipal \nCorporation \n8 \n13.08.2016, 07.05.2017, \n27.08.2017, 10.10.2019, \n20.08.2020, 03.10.2020, \n19.02.2021, 02.04.2022 \n7 Saraikela  \nSaraikela \nMuncipal \nCorporation \n7 \n21.08.2017, 20.08.2018, \n18.04.2018, 21.09.2019, \n11.01.2021, 21.08.2021, \n26.08.2022 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_229": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 229 \nANSWERED ON 22ndDECEMBER, 2022 \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n*229. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK:   \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n (a) whether each district has been included in the financial \nassistance given to Jharkhand under the Central Road and \nInfrastructure Fund (CRIF); \n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise; and \n(c) the targets set for development and maintenance of roads in the \nState in the coming three years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on Table of the House.  \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 229 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n22.12.2022, ASKED BY SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK  REGARDING \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n(a) AND (b) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways (MoRTH) \napproves works on the State Roads under the Central Road and \nInfrastructure Fund (CRIF) scheme as per recommendations of the \nconcerned State Government. Works on State Roads under CRIF are \napproved based on accrual under CRIF and proposals received from \nState Governments. The scheme earlier started as Central Road Fund \n(CRF) in the year 2000.  Since year 2000, this Ministry has approved  \n40 numbers of works amounting to Rs. 1368.64 Crores in the State of \nJharkhand. Out of total 24 districts of Jharkhand, CRIF works has \nbeen sanctioned in 17 districts.  The details of works approved on \nstate roads by MoRTH under CRF are at Annexure-I. \n(c) This Ministry is primarily responsible for development a nd \nmaintenance of the National Highways (NHs) in the country. MoRTH \nhas identified about 1573 km length of NHs with an estimated cost of \nRs.  39097 Crore for development of NHs during the next three years \nin Jharkhand state. Details are at Annexure-II.   \nMaintenance of the NHs is a continuous process which is taken \nup as per condition of the existing road, traffic density, inter -se \npriority and availability of funds to keep the NHs in the traffic-worthy \ncondition.   \n  \n\n  \n  \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) AND (b) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 229 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n22.12.2022, ASKED BY SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK  REGARDING \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n(Completed Road works under CRIF since year 2000 in Jharkhand) \n  \nSl No. \nName of work  District Cost       (Rs. \nin Crore) \n1 Widening & strengthening of Chaibasa-Jaintgarh Road from \nKM 35.00 to 58.00 in Hatgamharia Jaintgarh (Chaibasa-\nDiv.) \nWest \nSinghbhum 6.41 \n2 Widening & Strengthening of Gola-Muri road km 0.00 to \n27.00  (Hazaribagh-Div) \nRamgarh & \nRanchi 5.35 \n3 Widening &Strengthening of Balipur-Patla Bari from KM \n1.00 to 5.00 Chirkunda  Patchet Road KM 1.00 to 7.20 \n(Dhanbad-Div.) \nDhanbad 4.12 \n4 Widening & Strengthening of Khunti-Tamar road from \nKM  1.00 to 44.00 km (Kunti-Div) Khunti 9.91 \n5 Construction of Approaches and Protection  work to Jumar \nBridge in KM 119.00 of NH-33 (NH Ranchi -Div.) Ranchi 0.97 \n6 Strengthening of Daltonganj-Hariharganj Road from KM \n0.00 to 71.40 (Daltonganj-Div.) Palamu 12.41 \n7 Widening & Strengthening of Deoghar-Madhupur via \nMohanpur Road from KM 1.00 to 29.00 (Deoghar-Div.)  Deoghar 9.82 \n8 \nWidening & Strengthening of Kunti - Torpa Kolebira- Road \nfrom km 13.00 to 56.00 & 56 to 81  \nKhunti & \nGumla 33.16 \n9 Widening & Strengthening of Dhab-Pihra-Kalidih Road \n(Koderma Div) Koderma 11.43 \n10 Widening & Strengthening of Barabhoom-Bandoyan Road \nfrom km  0.00 to 10.80 (Jhamshedpur Div) East Singhbhum 8.80 \n11 Widening & Strengthening of Hata -Tring Road from km \n0.00 to 13.165(Jamshed pur-Div.) \nSaraikela-\nKharsawan 9.19 \n12 \nWidening & St