    "ls|LS17_S10_U_248": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 248 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08th December, 2022 \nEthanol Production Units \n†248. SHRI RAKESH SINGH: \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether ethanol production units have been set up as ethanol is likely to be mixed \nwith petrol and diesel in the country and if so, the details thereof, State-wise \nincluding Madhya Pradesh; \n(b) the quantity of ethanol is likely to be produced annually from these plants; \n(c) the details of petrol and diesel saved from the production of ethanol; \n(d) whether ethanol production units are proposed to be set up in Jabalpur;  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof including production capacity; and \n(f)  the progress made so far in this regard? \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000 वर तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL \nGAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a):  Entrepreneurs are free to set up ethanol production units in any part of the \ncountry including in Madhya Pradesh. The State-wise details of the ethanol \nproduction units including in Madhya Pradesh are at Annexure. \n(b): The current annual production capacity in the country for ethanol blending in \npetrol and other uses is about 947 crore litres. \n(c): As one litre of ethanol displaces one litre of petrol on volumetric basis, equivalent \nquantity of petrol is saved due to ethanol blending. At present there is no blending of \nethanol in diesel. \n(d) to (f): Two ethanol production units in Jabalpur District of Madhya Pradesh with \nan estimated annual ethanol production capacity of about 10.56 crore litre have been \nsanctioned financial assistance under Interest Subvention Scheme of Department of \nFood & Public Distribution. \n****** \n\n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION No. 248 FOR 08.12.2022 REGARDING ETHANOL PRODUCTION UNITS  \nState/UT \nNo. of \nMolasses \nBased \nprojects \nInstalled \nCapacity (in \nCr ltrs p.a.) \nNo. of Grain \nBased \nprojects \nInstalled \nCapacity (in \nCr ltrs p.a.) \nTotal \nInstalled \nCapacity (in \nCr ltrs p.a.) \nAndhra Pradesh 8 8.6 15 38.8 47.4 \nBihar 8 16.3 5 22.8 39.1 \nChhattisgarh 0 0 3 4.8 4.8 \nDaman & Diu 0 0 0 0 0 \nDelhi 0 0 0 0 0 \nGoa 0 0 0 0 0 \nGujarat 12 15.3 0 0 15.3 \nHaryana 4 8.6 15 49.1 57.6 \nHimachal Pradesh 0 0 3 2.7 2.7 \nJammu & Kashmir 0 0 0 0 0 \nJharkhand 0 0 2 12.4 12.4 \nKarnataka 33 100.2 6 18.5 118.7 \nMadhya Pradesh 3 4.8 8 18.0 22.8 \nMaharashtra 116 225.3 28 42.7 268.0 \nOdisha 0 0 2 3.1 3.1 \nPunjab 3 5.1 18 76.8 82.0 \nRajasthan 0 0 8 12.2 12.2 \nTamil Nadu 12 17.4 0 0 17.4 \nTelangana 3 6.8 2 7.3 14.1 \nUttar Pradesh 58 205.3 2 3.4 208.7 \nUttarakhand 3 5.8 0 0 5.8 \nWest Bengal 0 0 3 10.2 10.2 \nAndaman & \nNicobar 0 0 0 0 0 \nArunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 0 0 \nAssam 0 0 2 3.6 3.6 \nChandigarh 0 0 0 0 0 \nDadar & Nagar \nHaveli 0 0 0 0 0 \nKerala 0 0 0 0 0 \nLadakh 0 0 0 0 0 \nManipur 0 0 0 0 0 \nMeghalaya 0 0 0 0 0 \nMizoram 0 0 0 0 0 \nNagaland 0 0 0 0 0 \nPuducherry 0 0 0 0 0 \nSikkim 0 0 1 2.2 2.2 \nTripura 0 0 0 0 0 \nTotal 263 619.4 123 328.5 947.9 \nSource: Dept of Food & Public Distribution, Govt of India  ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_249": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 249 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON - 08.12.2022 \n \nREUSE OF WATER AFTER PURIFICATION \n \n249. SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN: \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \na.) whether as per the report of NITI Aayog  published recently, only five per cent of water is  \nreused in the country after purification and India is lagging far behind from other countries \nof the world in this regard; \nb.) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons for the same; \nc.) the steps taken/being taken by the Government  to preserve the depleting water reserves in \nthe country  and to reduce the disparity between population and  available water \nreserves/resources; \nd.) whether the Government is working on such  a technology through which maximum amount \nof used water can be purified for reuse and if so, the details thereof; \ne.) the details of other measures taken by the  Government to maximise the reuse of water after  \npurification; and \nf.) whether the Government has sanctioned and released any funds to the States in this regard \nand if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL)  \n(a) to (f) Government of India is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) – Har Ghar Jal, since \nAugust, 2019, in partnership with States, to make provision of potable tap water supply in \nadequate quantity, of prescribed quality and on regular & long -term basis to every rural \nhousehold by 2024. \nWater being a state subject , planning, approval and implementation of drinking water supply \nschemes, lies with state/UT governments. Water Supply/ Water & Sanitation/ Public Health \nEngineering Departments and/or parastatal organization of respective State Government/ UT \nAdministration, are   responsible   for   making provision of water supply and ensuring quality of  \n\n \n \n \nwater supplied in their respective State/UT. Government of India supplements the efforts of the \nStates by providing technical and financial assistance. \nGrey water treatment and its reuse is an important component while planning for drinking water \nschemes under JJM. Provision has been made for the preparation of Village Action Plan (VAP) \nby village community, which inter alia includes creation of grey water management \ninfrastructure by converging schemes such as SBM (G), MGNREGS, 15 th Finance Commission \ngrants to rural local bodies/ PRIs, MP & MLA Local Area Development Funds, District Mineral \nDevelopment Fund, CSR fund, etc. As such the details of financial outlay made b y the States, \nincluding progress of grey water management are not maintained at the Government of India \nlevel. \nFor technological solutions, a Technical Committee under the chairmanship of Principal \nScientific Advisor (PSA) to Government of India has been s et up to examine and recommend \nvarious innovations and water related technologies received from govt./ autonomous/ private \nentities, which can be used in providing potable tap water supply to every household. \nUrban Wastewater Scenario in India report 2022 of NITI Aayog mentioned that currently, India  \ngenerates 72,368  Million Litres per Day (MLD) of urban wastewater, and only 28% (20,236 \nMLD) is treated. \nNational Water Policy (2012) advocates the reuse of urban water effluents from kitchens and \nbathrooms, after primary treatment, in flush toilets should be encouraged, ensuring no human \ncontact. \nAs informed by Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs  (MoHUA), AMRUT 2.0 has been \nlaunched on 1st Oct 2021 covering all the statutory towns of the country to ensure u niversal \ncoverage of water supply & make cities ‘water secure’. It envisages rejuvenation of water bodies, \nurban aquifer management, promote recycle & reuse and rainwater harvesting to augment \nfreshwater resources.  \nAMRUT 2.0 supports treated water recycling and reuse projects for the non-drinking water needs \nsuch as industries, agricultural etc.  Reuse of treated used water, tertiary treatment with end -to-\nend reuse plan (preferably in PPP mode), provision/augmentation and rehabilitation of sewerage \nsystems with end-to-end treatment and reuse , identifying the bulk users of recycled used water \nand facilitating sale of used water to potential users  etc. are admissible elements under water \nsupply projects. \nNational Water Mission conducts mass awareness programmes like water talks, webinars, \ntrainings, seminars, workshops, exhibitions etc. from time to time to create awareness to promote \nwater conservation, rainwater harvesting, reuse and recycling of water. \nCentral Water Commission is monitoring live sto rage status of 143 reservoirs of the country on \nweekly basis. The total live storage capacity of these 143 reservoirs is 177.464 BCM which is \nabout 68.83% of the live storage capacity of 257.812 BCM which is estimated to have been \ncreated in the country. As on 01.12.2022, the live storage available in these reservoirs is 146.553 \nBCM, which is 83% of total live storage capacity of these reservoirs. \n \n\n \n \nThe other important steps taken for sustainable ground water management including checking \nthe depletion of g round water resources and its improvement in the country  are available at the \nfollowing URL:  \nhttp://jalshaktidowr.gov.in/sites/default/files/Steps%20taken%20by%20the%20Central%20Govt\n%20for%20water_depletion_july2022.pdf \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_25": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 25 \nANSWERED ON 8TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nWORK ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS-730C \n \n*25. SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR: \n                    \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the work of rehabilitation and upgradation of a two lane road \nwith paved shoulder configuration of Allahganj – Miranpur Katra \nsection on Bareilly -Farrukhabad-Madnapur-Katra-Jalalabad National \nHighway (NH) 730C is being undertaken;  \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has received requests from the public \nrepresentatives to investigate the alleged irregularities in the \nconstruction works and quality assessment of the building materials \nused in the construction works of the said NH by the Central Technical \nCommittee; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the action taken or likely to be taken by the government in this regard? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n  \n(a) to (e)   A STATEMENT is laid on the Table of the House. \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.25 ANSWERED ON 8TH DECEMBER, 2022 ASKED BY \nSHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR, REGARDING “WORK ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS-\n730C” \n \n(a) and (b) Yes Sir, The work of rehabilitation and upgradation to 2-lane with \npaved shoulders of Allahganj – Miranpur Katra section (km 52.770 to km \n114.000) of NH-730C in the State of Uttar Pradesh  has been taken up under \nGreen National Highways Corridor Project (GNHCP) with loan assistance of \nWorld Bank on EPC mode. The work has been awarded by Ministry at a  cost \nof Rs. 195.07 crore and the appointed date for the project has been fixed as \n08/07/2022.  Overall physical progress is about 20%. \n(c)  to (e)  Yes Sir,   the Government has received requests from the public \nrepresentatives regarding quality issues.  A   central independent expert has \nbeen appointed to investigate the alleged irregularities in the construction \nworks and quality assessment of the construction materials in the \nconstruction works. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_26": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nLPG Connections under PMUY \n \n†*26.  DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:  \nSHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of LPG connections provided under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \nsince the inception of the scheme till November, 2022, State/UT-wise, especially in the Jhalawar \nand Baran districts of Rajasthan, classified as active and inactive;  \n(b) the number of refillings done under Ujjwala Yojana during the last two years including in \nJhalawar and Baran districts of Rajasthan;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to run any campaign to increase this number and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n(d) the details of refillings done under the said scheme during the last one year in Madhya \nPradesh and Rajasthan categorized as one refill, two refills, three refills and four or more refills? \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000) \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d)   A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 BY DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN AND SHRI DUSHYANT \nSINGH TO BE ANSWERED ON 8 th DECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING ‘LPG \nCONNECTIONS UNDER PMUY’.  \n \n(a)State/UT-wise details of LPG connections released under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana(PMUY)  \nsince inception of the scheme till November, 2022 are at Annexure. Details of LPG connections \nreleased under PMUY since inception of the scheme till November, 2022 in the districts of Jhalawar \nand Baran of Rajasthan are as under :-  \nDistrict Number of LPG connections released \nunder PMUY (in lakh) \nJhalawar 2.03 \nBaran 1.60 \n \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \nAll PMUY connections are in active status \n(b)   Details of number of refills consumed by PMUY beneficiaries in 2020-21 and 2021-22 across \nthe country  including Jhalawar and Baran  districts of Rajasthan are as under :-  \nDistrict Number of  refills (in terms of 14.2 kg refills)  (in lakh) \n2020-21 2021-22 \nJhalawar 9.37 8.98 \nBaran 5.96 5.04 \nRajasthan State 277.52 267.27 \nAll India 3511.47 3126.23 \n \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n \n(c)  To improve LPG usage amongst PMUY beneficiaries, Government and Oil Marketing \nCompanies (OMCs) have organized LPG panchayats, social media campaigns, public events for the \ndistribution of connections and public awareness campaigns etc. across the country. Conducting LPG \nPanchayats across the country is an ongoing process and so far 381 and 285 LPG Panchayats have \nbeen conducted in Baran and Jalawar districts of Rajasthan respectively. \nFurther, Government has taken several other steps to encourage consumption of LPG by PMUY \nbeneficiaries, which include subsidy of ₹ 200/- per 14.2 Kg refill upto 12 refills/year for PMUY \nbeneficiaries for year 2022-23, option of 5 Kg Double Bottle Connection (DBC), swap option from \n14.2 Kg to 5 Kg, upto 3 free refills to PMUY beneficiaries under Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan \nPackage from April to December 2020 etc. \n(d)   As on 31.03.2021, there were 71.43 lakh  and 63.59 lakh PMUY beneficiaries in the States of \nMadhya Pradesh and Rajasthan respectively. Details of number of  PMUY customers who have taken \none refill, two refills, three refills, four or more refills during the last year i.e. 2021-22 in the States of \nMadhya Pradesh and Rajasthan are as under :-  \nState one refill \n(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nTwo refills \n(including \ninstallation refill (in \nlakh) \nthree refills \n(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nFour or more \nrefills(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nMadhya Pradesh 18.85 15.74 11.54 22.84 \nRajasthan 7.64 10.68 11.49 33.02 \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n------- \n\nAnnexure  \nAnnexure referred in part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 26  to be answered on \n08.12.2022 asked by Dr. Dhal Singh Bisen and Shri  Dushyant Singh regarding \"  LPG \nConnections under PMUY” \nState/UT \nTotal number of LPG connections released \nunder  PMUY as on 30.11.2022 \nAndaman and Nicobar Islands 13,447 \nAndhra Pradesh 5,11,579 \nArunachal Pradesh 49,118 \nAssam 43,79,144 \nBihar 1,07,10,910 \nChandigarh 562 \nChhattisgarh 34,90,375 \nDadar & Nagar Havelly and Daman \n& Diu 15,016 \nDelhi 1,41,042 \nGoa 1,147 \nGujarat 38,40,339 \nHaryana 7,66,461 \nHimachal Pradesh 1,40,672 \nUT of Jammu and Kashmir 12,44,016 \nJharkhand 36,42,058 \nKarnataka 37,48,565 \nKerala 3,41,040 \nUT of Ladakh 11,091 \nLakshadweep 310 \nMadhya Pradesh 82,20,240 \nMaharashtra 48,76,309 \nManipur 2,00,398 \nMeghalaya 2,09,800 \nMizoram 32,734 \nNagaland 90,464 \nOdisha 53,19,513 \nPuducherry 14,821 \nPunjab 12,83,863 \nRajasthan 69,24,719 \nSikkim 13,667 \nTamil Nadu 36,98,293 \nTelangana 11,52,103 \nTripura 2,81,825 \nUttar Pradesh 1,74,88,703 \nUttarakhand 4,97,374 \nWest Bengal 1,23,45,981 \nAll India TOTAL 9,56,97,699 \n \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_27": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 27 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  8th December 2022 )  \nNEW AIRPORTS UNDER RCS \n27.    SHRI DILIP SAIKIA  \n                       \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government proposes to start twenty airports, heliports \nand water aerodromes under the  Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS) \nby the year 2024-25; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Assam; \n(c) the names of the ports i dentified for the purpose and the present \nstatus thereof; \n(d) the time by which the said airports are likely to be set up; \n(e) whether air travel from Mangaldoi is proposed to be started under \nthe scheme and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) the time by which the flights are likely to be started therefrom? \nANSWER \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION      (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia) \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 27 \nREGARDING \"NEW AIRPORTS UNDER RCS\" TO BE ANSWERED ON \n08.12.2022.; ; (a) to (d): Details of the 68 identified aerodromes for \nrevival/upgradation under UDAN by 2024, are at Annexure.   The \nairports/heliports/water aerodromes identified for revival/upgradation \nunder UDAN in the State of Assam are Guwahati River Front (WA), \nNagaon (H), Rupsi and Umrangso Reservoir (WA).  ; (e) & (f): Mangaldoi \nairstrip is not available in the UDAN scheme document. However, if the \n\nState Government of Assam requests to include the same under UDAN, \nit shall be considered as per the provisions of scheme document. ; ; The \nSelected Airline Operators (SAOs) under UDAN are obligated to \ncommence RCS flight operations on a RCS route within a period of six \nmonths from the issuance of Letter of Award or within two months from \nthe readiness of airport, whichever is later, unless any extension is \ngranted by the Implementing Agency.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 27 for answer on 08.12.2022 \n \nList of 68 identified RCS Aerodrome, to operationalise by 2024 \nS. No. Name of State Airport Name Airport/Heliport \n(H)/ Water \nAerodrome \n(WA) \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar Campbell Bay Airport \n2.  Andaman & Nicobar Car Nicobar Airport \n3.  Andaman & Nicobar Havelock (W) WA \n4.  Andaman & Nicobar Long Island (W) WA \n5.  Andaman & Nicobar Neil Island (W) WA \n6.  Andaman & Nicobar Shibpur Airport \n7.  Andhra Pradesh Parkasam Barrage (W) WA \n8.  Arunachal Pradesh Daparizo (H) Heliport \n9.  Arunachal Pradesh Itanagar (H) Heliport \n10.  Arunachal Pradesh Tuting (H) Heliport \n11.  Arunachal Pradesh Walong (H) Heliport \n12.  Arunachal Pradesh Yinghoing (H) Heliport \n13.  Arunachal Pradesh Ziro (H) Heliport \n14.  Assam Guwahati River Front (W) WA \n15.  Assam Nagaon (H) Heliport \n16.  Assam Umrangso Reservoir (W) WA \n17.  Chhattisgarh Ambikapur Airport \n18.  Daman&Diu Daman Airport \n19.  Haryana Ambala Airport \n20.  Himachal Pradesh Baddi (H) Heliport \n21.  Himachal Pradesh Manali (H) Heliport \n22.  Himachal Pradesh Shimla(H) Heliport \n23.  Jharkhand Bokaro Airport \n24.  Jharkhand Dumka Airport \n25.  Jharkhand Hazaribagh Airport \n26.  Jharkhand Jamshedpur Airport \n27.  Ladakh Kargil Airport \n\n28.  Ladakh Thoise Airport \n29.  Lakshadweep Kavaratti WA \n30.  Lakshadweep Minicoy WA \n31.  Maharashtra Amravati Airport \n32.  Maharashtra Ratnagiri Airport \n33.  Maharashtra Solapur Airport \n34.  Manipur Jiribam (H) Heliport \n35.  Manipur Moreh (H) Heliport \n36.  Manipur Parbung (H) Heliport \n37.  Manipur Tamenglong (H) Heliport \n38.  Manipur Thanlon (H) Heliport \n39.  Odisha Rourkela Airport \n40.  Odisha Utkela Airport \n41.  Rajasthan Kota Airport \n42.  Rajasthan Uterlai Airport \n43.  Tamil Nadu Neyveli Airport \n44.  Tamil Nadu Ramnad Airport \n45.  Tamil Nadu Tanjore Airport \n46.  Tamil Nadu Vellore Airport \n47.  Telangana NagarjunaSagar (W) WA \n48.  Uttar Pradesh Aligarh Airport \n49.  Uttar Pradesh Ayodhya (Faizabad) Airport \n50.  Uttar Pradesh Azamgarh Airport \n51.  Uttar Pradesh Chitrakoot Airport \n52.  Uttar Pradesh Fursatganj Airport \n53.  Uttar Pradesh Ghazipur Airport \n54.  Uttar Pradesh Jhansi Airport \n55.  Uttar Pradesh Meerut  Airport \n56.  Uttar Pradesh Moradabad Airport \n57.  Uttar Pradesh Muirpur (Korba) Airport \n58.  Uttar Pradesh Sarsawa (Saharanpur) Airport \n59.  Uttar Pradesh Shravasti Airport \n60.  Uttarakhand Dharchula (H) Heliport \n\n61.  Uttarakhand Joshimath (H) Heliport \n62.  Uttarakhand Mussoorie (H) Heliport \n63.  Uttarakhand Nainital (H) Heliport \n64.  Uttarakhand Haridwar (H) Heliport \n65.  Uttarakhand Ramnagar (H) Heliport \n66.  West Bengal Burnpur Airport \n67.  West Bengal Cooch Behar Airport \n68.  West Bengal Kalaikunda Airport \n \nThe above list of 68 RCS Aerodromes is as on 30.11.2022. \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_28": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 28\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nNEW FLIGHTS TO LAKSHADWEEP\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has received any proposal from other airline\ncompanies except Air India to start new flights to Lakshadweep;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether the Government  has taken further initiatives to expand air\ntravels to promote tourism in Lakshadweep and if so,  the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****\n*28. SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.\n28 REGARDING \"NEW FLIGHTS TO LAKSHADWEEP\" TO BE\nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022.\n \n(a) to (c): As per ongoing Winter Schedule-2022, M/s Alliance Air is operating\n7 flights per week on Kochi-Agatti route. With the repeal of the Air\nCorporation Act,1953, in March 1994, the Indian domestic aviation market\nwas deregulated. Consequently, airlines are free to induct capacity with any\naircraft type for selecting whatever markets and networks they wish to service\nand operate across the country. Thus, it is up to the airlines to provide air\nservices to specific places depending upon the traffic demand and their\ncommercial viability, in compliance with the relevant guidelines in this\nmatter.\n \nMinistry of Civil Aviation has launched Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS)\n- UDAN (Ude Deshka Aam Nagrik) on 21-10-2016 to stimulate regional air\nconnectivity and making air travel affordable to the masses which includes\nairports/heliports/water aerodromes.In order to expand air travels to promote\ntourism in Lakshadweep, Ministry of Civil Aviation introduced a new mode of\ntransportation i.e. seaplane operations from water aerodromes under the\nUDAN scheme. \n \nTill the completion of four rounds of bidding, twenty four (24) routes\ninvolving eight (8) water aerodromes have been identified/awarded in\nLakshadweep (UT) for the seaplane operation which are Kavaratti, Minicoy,\nAgatti, Kalpeni, Bitra, Kadmat, Kiltan, & Bangaram. \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_29": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 29\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nGREENFIELD AIRPORT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has recently inaugurated country’s first\nGreenfield ‘Donyi Polo’ airport in Arunachal Pradesh;\n(b) if so, the details and the salient features thereof;\n(c) the details of the proposals received for setting up of Greenfield Airports\nfrom the State Governments along with the NOC given by Union Government\nfor the purpose during each of the last three years and the current year,\nState/UT-wise;\n(d) the details of the criteria fixed by the Government for setting up of\nGreenfield Airports in the country; and\n(e) the details of the funds allocated and utilized for setting up of Greenfield\nAirports during the said period?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n (a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*29. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\nSHRI RAVI KISHAN\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement in reply to parts (a) to (e) of the Lok Sabha Starred (*) Question\nNo.29 for answer on 08.12.2022 regarding \"Greenfield Airport \"\n \n(a) & (b): Donyi Polo Airport, Itanagar,  the first Greenfield Airport of\nArunachal Pradesh has been inaugurated on 19.11.2022. The Donyi Polo\nairport has a Terminal Building capable of handling 300 passengers during\npeak hours. With a 2300 meter long runway, the airport is suitable for the\noperation of B-737/A-320 type of aircraft. Along with a suitable apron for\nparking of three B-737/A-320 type of aircraft, the airport also has  a special\napron to accommodate four Mi-17 helicopters. The airport is operational\nsince 28.11.2022.\n \n(c) & (d):  Development of a Greenfield airport is governed under the\nGreenfield Airports (GFA) Policy, 2008. As per the Policy, a State\nGovernment or an Airport Developer, willing to establish an airport, is\nrequired to send a proposal to the Ministry of Civil Aviation (MoCA) for 2-\nstage approval i.e. 'Site-Clearance' followed by 'In-Principle' approval. Such\nproposals are considered by MoCA as per the procedure stipulated in the\nGFA Policy.\n \nAs of now, Government of India (GoI) has accorded 'In-Principle' approval\nfor setting up of 21 Greenfield Airports across the country. Out of these, 9\nGreenfield airports viz. Durgapur, Shirdi, Kannur, Pakyong, Kalaburagi,\nOrvakal (Kurnool), Sindhudurg, Kushinagar and Donyi Polo, Itanagar have\nbeen operationalized.\n \nGovernment of India has also granted the first stage clearance i.e. site\nclearance for construction of three Greenfield airports namely Alwar in\nRajasthan, Singrauli in Madhya Pradesh and Mandi in Himachal Pradesh.\nDuring the last  three years, Mandi Airport project has been granted 'Site\nClearance' in 2022.\n \n(e): The project cost of nine (09) operationalized airports is: Durgapur-Rs.670\ncrore, Shirdi-Rs.320 crore, Pakyong- Rs. 553.53 core, Kannur- Rs.2342 crore,\nKalaburagi- Rs.175.57 crore, Orvakal  (Kurnool)- Rs.187 crore, Sindhudurg-\nRs.520 crore, Kushinagar-Rs.448 crore and Donyi Polo, Itanagar-Rs.646\ncrore.\n \nAs per Greenfield Airports Policy, the responsibility of implementation of\nairport projects including funding of the projects rests with the concerned\nairport developer including the respective State Government (in case the State\nGovernment is the project proponent). \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_3": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 03 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nEXPORT OF BROKEN RICE \n \n03.  SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री)be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)   whether it is not true that there has been a huge  demand for broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo-political reasons; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; \n(c)   whether it is also not true that export of broken  rice has increased by more than 40 \ntimes in the last four years and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether 21.03 lakh tonnes of broken rice was  exported between April -August, 2022; \nand \n(e)   if so, the reasons for banning broken rice exports from the country? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a) & (b):  Yes Sir. There has been sudden increase in demand for Indian broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo -political reasons. India’s exports of broken rice have increased \nfrom 17.86 Lakh MT in 2021 -22(April- September) to 23.82 Lakh MT in 2022 -23 (April -\nSeptember) registering a growth of 33.37%.  \n \n(c):  No Sir. The export of broken rice from India has increased about three time s in the last \nfour years i.e from 12.21 Lakh MT in 2018 -19 to 38.90 Lakh MT in 2021 -22 in volume and in \nvalue terms i.e USD 369.58 Million in 2018 -19 to USD 1132.94 Million in 2021 -22. However, \n\n2 \n \nexport of broken rice witnessed a decline in 2019 -20 due to th e pandemic and domestic policies \nof major importing countries in Africa. The details are given in the Table below: - \n \nYear Quantity in MT Value in Million USD \n2018-19 12,21,617 369.58 \n2019-20 2,70,338 82.48 \n2020-21 20,64,562 595.69 \n2021-22 38,90,866 1132.94 \n2021-22 \n(April-September) \n17,86,435 516.89 \n2022-23 * \n(April-September) \n23,81,657 764.81 \nSource: DGCIS, Kolkata.   \n* Figures for 2022-23 are provisional and subject to change. \n \n(d): A total of 21.64 Lakh MT of broken rice was exported from India during the period \nbetween April-August in 2022. \n \n(e):   Broken rice is majorly used for ethanol production and as poultry & cattle feed.  As \nevident from Table at Para -(c) above, there has been sudden rise in demand for Indian broken \nrice due to geo -political situation around. Further, as per the first Advance Estimates for 2022 -\n23,released by Directorate of Economics and Statistics, Department of Agriculture and Farmers’ \nWelfare, the production of rice is estimated to be lower than the target fixed for the  current year. \nTherefore, in order to ensure adequate availability of broken rice in domestic market,the \nGovernment has prohibited export of broken rice w.e.f 9thSeptember, 2022.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_30": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *30 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \nDPR OF INTER-LINKING OF RIVERS \n*30  SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has prepared draft DPR f or five river linking projects in the country and \nmentioned in the recent budget 2022-23 that once th e consensus is reached among the beneficiary States , \nthe Government would provide for its implementation and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether any States have specifically objected o r have expressed any reservations which are causing  \ndelay in implementation of these river linking projects and if so, the details thereof including the nature of \nthe objections; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to include in t hese five draft DPR the other pending projects on t he \nrespective rivers which are going to be linked toge ther including river-basin area relating to ground water \nrecharge/conservation/irrigation project(s) especially for Par-Tapi-Narmada river linking project; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI    \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*30 TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 REGARDING “DPR OF INTER-LINKING OF \nRIVERS”.  \n(a) & (b)  The five river linking projects for which draft Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) were completed, \nas mentioned in the speech of Union Minister of Fin ance on 01.02.2022 for Union Budget for financial \nyear 2022-23, include Damanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tap i-Narmada link projects apart from the three \ncomponents of the Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Gr and Anicut) link project, viz; Godavari \n(Inchampalli)-Krishna (Nagarjunasagar), Krishna (Na garjunasagar)-Pennar (Somasila) and Pennar \n(Somasila)-Cauvery (Grand Anicut) links.These links  projects are among the 30 link projects identified  \nunder the National Perspective Plan (NPP) prepared by the Government of India. \nDamanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tapi-Narmada link project s are twin link projects concerning the States of \nMaharashtra and Gujarat.  DPR of Damanganga-Pinjal link was circulated to the States in 2014 and \naccorded techno-economic clearance in 2016 while re vised DPR  of Par-Tapi-Narmada link was \ncirculated to the States in 2017. Various consultat ion meetings have been held by the Government of \nIndia.  \nAs envisaged under the NPP, Godavari is to be augme nted by transfer of surplus waters from Mahanadi \nand Brahmaputra basins, for further diversion of wa ters to Krishna, Pennar and Cauvery basins. Pending  \nconsensus on Mahanadi-Godavari and Godavari (Inchampalli)-Krishna link projects, alternative studies to \ndivert surplus waters available in Godavari basin and unutilized waters in Indravati sub-basin of Godavari \nbasin through Godavari-Cauvery link project were ca rried out by National Water Development Agency \n(NWDA). This link project traverses through Godavar i, Krishna, Pennar, Palar and Cauvery basins and \nlies in the States of Telangana, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.  \nThe DPRs of Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Grand An icut) link project were prepared in consultation \nwith the party States and circulated to them in Apr il, 2021. The party States have raised concerns rel ated \nto surplus waters availability in Godavari basin an d quantum of allocation of waters to the States. \nConsultation meetings with party States/Union Terri tory (UT) have been held and as decided during the \nmeeting held on 18.02.2022, a Technical Feasibility  Report (TFR) has also been prepared by NWDA to \nrecast the proposal limiting the transfer of waters  from 7,000 Million Cubic Metres (MCM) to about \n4,000 MCM from Godavari basin, in the interim phase , along with combining the proposal for \nsupplementation in Krishna basin through Bedti-Vard a link. The consultation meeting was last held on \n18.10.2022 and efforts made for the party States to  arrive at a consensus for implementation of the li nk \nproject. \nThe implementation of the link projects depends upon the party States to arrive at a consensus. \n  \n(c) & (d)  No such proposal is under consideration. \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_31": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *31\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n08.12.2022\n \n \nSTARTUP COMPANIES\n \n \n*31.        \nSHRI S. VENKATESAN:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it has come to the notice of the Government that 90 to 95 per \ncent of startup companies have failed;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the percentage of failures among Micro, Small and M\nedium \nEnterprises;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the statistics with the Government corroborate with the findings \nof these studies on failure of startups; and\n \n(d) \n \nif not, the failure rate as per the details available with the Government? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL\n \nAND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) \nto (d): \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *31 FOR 08.12.2022 REGARDING STARTUP \nCOMPANIES\n \n \n \n(a) \nto (d): \n \nAs per Allocation of Business Rules, 1961, matters relating to \nstartups come under the purview of the Department for Promotion of Industry \nand Internal Trade (DPIIT), Ministry of Commerce and Industry. As per the \ninformation provided by DPIIT, the Governme\nnt of India has taken a \ncomprehensive approach under the Startup initiative which has resulted in the \ncountry's Startup ecosystem maturing into the third largest startup ecosystem\n. \nT\nhe Government has \nalready \nrecognized \nmore than 84\n,000 Startups that are \nsp\nread over 640 districts with 45% of them\n \nbeing from\n \ntier 2 and 3 cities. The \nrecognized Startups have reported creation of over \n8.4\n \nlakh jobs. Further, more \nthan 100 India Startups have achieved unicorn status (entity valued over USD l \nbillion) as \non\n \ndate.\n \n \n \nWhereas, r\negular businesses \nare often\n \nmeasured by success or failure in a \nspecific number of years of operation\n,\n \nStartups and scale ups are more \naccurately measured by failure or success in a particular phase\n \nmaking it \ndifficult to put together a statis\ntic covering all types of new businesses and their \nfailure rate with any level of accuracy.\n \n \n \n \nThe information with respect to the failure of Startups is not centrally \nmaintained by the Government. \n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n ",
