    "ls|LS17_S10_U_33": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.12.2022\n \n \nASPIRE\n \n \n*33. \n \nSHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it is true that A Scheme for Promotion of Innovation, Rural Industries and \nEntrepreneurship (ASPIRE) has shown accelerated growth in the agro\n-\nbased industry in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the number and the details of Incubation Centres establis\nhed so far, State\n-\nwise \nincluding Uttar Pradesh; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether Incubation Centres help the startups and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) to (c):\n \nA statement is laid on the \nTable of the House.\n \n \n \n\n \n  \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE PARTS (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.*33 FOR ANSWERON 08.12.2022\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nA Scheme for Promotion of Innovation, Rural Industries and Entrepreneurship \n(ASPIRE) was launched on 16.03.2015 with an objective of setting up a network of Livelihood \nBusiness Incubators (LBIs) and Technology Business Incubators (TBIs) to create new jobs \nand \nreduce unemployment, promote entrepreneurship and innovation in agro rural sector. \n \nLBI focuses on imparting training and handholding support to local youth for job creation through \ncreation of micro enterprises and wage employment. Till 30.09.2022, \n65\n,467 beneficiaries have \nbeen trained in 81 LBIs, of which 16,338 trainees got self\n-\nemployed and 9,874 trainees got \nemployed in agro rural sector. TBI focuses on providing incubation support to aspiring \nentrepreneurs and startups for product prototyping and\n \ncommercialization in agro\n-\nrural sector\n. \nTill 30.09.2022, through 14 TBIs, 98 Startups have been incubated with over 100 \nproducts/services commercialized in the agro rural sector. \n \nBesides, ASPIRE has a Fund of Funds component to support, through various A\nlternate \nInvestment Funds, investment in early stage MSME enterprises and startups in agro rural \nsector to \nimprove manufacturing of products, supply chain, technology adoption and \ndiversification, market penetration, etc. Till 30.11.2022, under ASPIRE Fund\n \nof Funds, 05 \nAIFs have invested Rs. 579.33 cr. in 91 agro rural based enterprises leading to an increase in \nturnover by Rs. 4278.77 cr. and creation of 6537 new jobs.\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nThe number and details of incubation centres setup under the schemes of \nGovernment of I\nndia, State\n-\nwise including Uttar Pradesh, are given in \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nTechnology Business Incubators (TBIs) under ASPIRE assist agro rural Startups in \ndeveloping and commercializing new technologies through incubation support by providing \nnecessary infrastr\nucture such as office space, access to mentors and finance, value added \nservices like IPR, market linkages, etc.  The details of Startups supported by ASPIRE TBIs are \ngiven in \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n.\n \n \nUnder the revised guidelines for LBIs, focus is given on providing support to aspiring \nentrepreneurs for setting up of formal (registered) micro\n-\nenterprises through incubation \nprogrammes and support services such as access to finance, guidance on regulator\ny licenses, \nGoods & Services Tax (GST) registration, access to vendors, etc. Accordingly, a provision has \nbeen made for supporting the LBIs with Operational Expenditure for hiring of dedicated staff, \nrunning outreach programmes, etc.\n \n \nIn addition, Governme\nnt of India provides incubation support through following schemes\n-\n \ni\n.\n \nRashtriya\n \nKrishi\n \nVikas\n \nYojana\n-\n \nRemunerative Approach for Agriculture and Allied sector \nRejuvenation (\nRKVY\n-\nRAFTAAR), Ministry of Agriculture and Farmer Welfare: Under \nthis scheme, Agribusiness Incubators are setup, which in turn provides mentoring and \nseed stage funding to Startups in agriculture and allied sectors. A total of 24 RKVY \nAgribusiness Incubato\nrs (R\n-\nABIs) have been established and \n1055\n \nstart\n-\nups \nhave \nbeen \nselected \nfor\n \nfinancial\n \nassistance\n. (Details given in \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n)\n \n \nii\n.\n \nNational Initiative for Developing and Harnessing Innovation (NIDHI) scheme, \nDepartment of Science & Technology; Under\n \nthe scheme, TBIs are established to \nprovide support to technology startups by the way of physical workspace, mentoring, \nlegal support, IP/Patent support, etc. A total of 159 TBIs have been established till date. \n(Details given in \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n)\n \n \n \n \n\n \n  \n \n \niii\n.\n \nPradhan \nMantri Formalization of Micro Food Processing Enterprises (PMFME) \nScheme, Ministry of Food Processing Industries: The scheme \nprovides support for \nsetting up Incubation Centres which impart training to \nmicro food processing \nentrepreneurs, Farmer Producer Or\nganizations (FPOs), Self Help Groups, Cooperatives, \netc. These Incubation Centres \ncan also be utilized as common food processing facilities\n \nby \nthe entrepreneurs on hiring basis. A total of 76 Incubation Centres have been approved \nunder the scheme till date\n. \n(Details given in \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n)\n \niv\n.\n \nAtal Innovation Mission (AIM), NITI Aayog: Atal Innovation Mission supports \nestablishment of incubation centres through its components\n-\nAtal Incubation Centres (AICs) \nand Atal Community Innovation Centres (ACICs). AICs and AC\nICs focus on creating \nworld class incubation facilities across various parts of India with suitable physical \ninfrastructure in terms of equipment and operating facilities, mentoring support through \nsectoral experts, business planning support, access to see\nd capital, industry partners, \ntrainings, etc. in various sectors such as manufacturing, transport, energy, health, \neducation, agriculture, water and sanitation, SDG etc. Till date, 69 AICs and 14 ACICs \nhave been established/operationalized. (Details given \nin \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n)\n \n \n*****\n\n \n  \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI referred to in reply to part (b) and (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. *33 for answer on 08.12.2022\n \nState wise \ndetails of established Livelihood Business Incubators and Technology \nBusiness Incubators\n \nLivelihood Business Incubators (LBIs)\n \nSr.No.\n \nState/UT\n \nDistrict/City\n \nName of LBI\n \nThrust Area \n \n1\n.\n \n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \nRajahmundry\n \n \nCoir Board, Rajahmundry, \nAndhra Pradesh\n \nCoir Based Multi \nProduct\n \n2\n.\n \n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \nEast Godavari\n \nAditya College of \nEngineering & Technology\n \nFood Processing\n-\n \nBeverages, Bakery, \nMasala and Fruits & \nVegetable Processing\n \n3\n.\n \n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \nKrishna\n \nIndian Institute of \nPlantation& Management, \nVijayawada,\n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \nAgriculture & Allied \n(Vermi\n-\ncompost, Value \nAddition Seed Bank)\n \n4\n.\n \n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \nChittoor\n \nSPMVV Livelihood \nbusiness Incubation \nfacility. Chittoor, Andhra \nPradesh\n \nFood Processing; \nAgriculture & Allied; \nFashion Technology \n \n5\n.\n \n \nAssam\n \nKamrup\n \nMetro/Guwahati\n \nIndian Institute of \nEntrepreneurship, \nGuwahati, Assam\n \nWood based\n \n6\n.\n \n \nAssam\n \nKamrup\n \nIndian Institute of \nEntrepreneurship, Kamrup, \nAssam \n \nFruit & Vegetable\n \n7\n.\n \n \nAssam\n \nSivasagar\n \nIndian Institute of \nEntrepreneurship, \nSivasagar, Assam\n \nDairy\n \n8\n.\n \n \nBihar\n \nNawada\n \nNSIC, Technical Services \nCentre, Nawada, Bihar\n \nBakery, packaging, \nspice grinding, stitching \n& embroidery, beauty \nparlor, mobile repairing \n \n9\n.\n \n \nBihar\n \nNawada (Khanwa)\n \nBhartiya Harit Khadi \nGramodaya Sansthan \n(BHKGS)\n-\n \nKVIC, \nKhanwa, Nawada