�������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n\n�\n������ �������� ���������� ���� ������� ����� �� �������������� ����� ��������\n����� ���� ������ ������ ��� �������������� ��������� ���� ������������ ���� ���������\n��������������������\n�\n����� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������\n�\n���� � ���� �\n��� � �������������� �\n�����\n�������� �\n������\n���������\n�����\n�������� �\n������\n���������\n��\n���� �\n��� �������� � �������� � �������� ��������\n��\n���� �\n��� �������� � �������� � �������� �������� �\n��\n���� �\n��� �������� � �������� � �������� � �������� �\n���� ���������������������������������������\n��� �������������������������������������\n��\n�\n�\n�\n�����\n�\n�\n�\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S10_U_51": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.51 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 9TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nAYUSH CENTRES \n \n*51.SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI: \n       SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the State -wise details of number of AYUSH Health and Wellness Centres/Hospitals in the \ncountry along with the number of National level Institutes/ Health Centres in Maharashtra and \nMadhya Pradesh;  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to expand the facilities at these Centres; \n(c) the manner in which the Government has conceptualized setting up of AYUSH Centres for \nimproving the health and wellness of the citizens;  \n(d) the manner in which the AYUSH scheme has been implemented in Vidarbha and Marathwad a \nregions of Maharashtra and the achievements made so far;  \n(e) whether the Government plans to promote AYUSH systems of medicine internationally along \nwith the steps taken in this regard; and  \n(f) whether the Government proposes to open new AYUSH Centres in the country including tribal \nareas of Madhya Pradesh and if so, the State/ UT-wise details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n2 \n \nTHE STATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.51 FOR 9TH DECEMBER, 2022. \n(a) The State -wise details of number of AYUSH Hospitals and AYUSH Health and Wellness \nCenters as approved under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM) in \nthe country is furnis hed in Annexure -I. Further, National Institute of Naturopathy (NIN), Pune, an \nautonomous organization under Ministry of Ayush is functioning in the State of Maharashtra. \nHowever, no National level Institutes/Health Centers is functioning in the State of Madhya Pradesh \nunder control of Ministry of Ayush. \n(b) Government of India is implementing Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH \nMission (NAM) through State/UT Governments and supporting their efforts to expand the facilities \nin these centers through different activities like upgradation of existing AYU SH dispensaries & sub \nhealth centers as AYUSH Health and Wellness Centers, as well as upgradation of AYUSH \nHospitals, by providing infrastructure, supply of essential AYUSH Drugs and deployment of \nmanpower. State/UT Governments may avail the eligible financial assistance by submitting suitable \nproposals through State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs) as per the NAM guidelines.  \n \n(c)  The AYUSH systems advocate the holistic wellness approach aiming at prevention of diseases \nand promotion of health and wellbeing. T he main objectives are to establish a holistic wellness \nmodel based on AYUSH principles and practices, to empower masses for “self -care” to reduce the \ndisease burden, out of pocket expenditure and to provide informed choice to needy public. The \nhealth care facilities to be upgraded as AYUSH HWCs are AYUSH dispensaries and sub health \ncentres. The Ministry of Ayush has been given the task of developing 12,500 HWCs in a phased \nmanner in consultation with States/UTs and Ministry of Health & Family Welfare. \n  \n(d) Under NAM, 177 AYUSH Health and Wellness Centers have been approved in Vidarbha and \n151 are functional. Similarly, 30 AYUSH Health and Wellness Centers have been approved in \nMarathwada region and 25 are functional.  \n(e) The Ministry has developed a Central Sector Scheme for the Promotion of International Co -\noperation in AYUSH (IC Scheme) under which the Ministry of Ayush provides support to Indian \nAYUSH Manufacturers/ AYUSH Service providers to give boost to the export of AYUSH products \n& services; facilitates the International promotion, development and recognition of AYUSH system \nof medicine; foster interaction of stakeholders and market development of AYUSH at international \nlevel; promote academics and research through the establishment of AYUSH Ac ademic Chairs in \nforeign countries and holding training workshop/symposiums for promoting and strengthening \nawareness and interest about AYUSH Systems of Medicine at international level. \n(f) Public Health being a State subject, the opening of new AYUSH  centers comes under the \npurview of respective State/UT Governments including tribal area of Madhya Pradesh. However, \nunder the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM), there is a provision \nof financial assistance for setting up of 50 /30 /1 0 bedded integrated AYUSH hospitals in the \ncountry including Madhya Pradesh which also covers the tribal areas. The details of units approved \nin the country including Madhya Pradesh for setting up of upto 50 bedded integrated AYUSH \nhospitals from 2014-15 to 2021-22 is furnished at Annexure-II. \n\n3 \n \n  \nAnnexure I \n(i) State/UT-wise AYUSH Health and Wellness Centres (AHWC) approved and No. of AYUSH \nHWCs functional under NAM \nSr. No. Name of State/UT No. of approved \nAYUSH HWCs \n \nNo. of functional \nAYUSH HWCs \n \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Island 6 6 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 110 0 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 49 34 \n4 Assam 89 44 \n5 Bihar 268 18 \n6 Chandigarh 12 5 \n7 Chhattisgarh 240 240 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & \nDiu \n0 0 \n9 Delhi 0 0 \n10 Goa 40 9 \n11 Gujarat 265 223 \n12 Haryana 569 361 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 740 240 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 442 317 \n15 Jharkhand 267 115 \n16 Karnataka 376 176 \n17 Kerala 520 180 \n18 Ladakh 14 0 \n19 Lakshadweep 7 5 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 562 362 \n21 Maharashtra 294 281 \n22 Manipur 17 3 \n23 Meghalaya 45 0 \n24 Mizoram 38 24 \n25 Nagaland 49 4 \n26 Odisha 250 90 \n27 Puducherry 4 4 \n28 Punjab 217 0 \n29 Rajasthan 1000 484 \n30 Sikkim 18 18 \n31 Tamil Nadu 350 250 \n32 Telangana 421 421 \n33 Tripura 84 0 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 1034 500 \n35 Uttarakhand 300 70 \n36 West Bengal 271 271 \n Total 8968 4755 \n \n \n \n \n\n4 \n \n \n(ii) State